===Verb===
{{en-verb|draws|drawing|drew|drawn|past_ptc2=drew|past_ptc2_qual=colloquial and nonstandard}}

# To pull or exert force.
## To [[pull]] (something) in a particular manner or direction. {{defdate|from 8th c.}}
##: {{usex|en|He '''drew''' a sheaf of papers from his bag.}}
## To [[drag]] (a person, thing, or part of the body), especially along the ground. {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1697|author=w:William Dampier|title=A New Voyage Round the World
|passage=Seals {{...}} throw their bodies forward, '''drawing''' their hinder-parts after them.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[pull]] (a plough, vehicle etc.); to cause (something) to move forwards by pulling it. {{defdate|from 11th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Grafton Chronicle|volume=II|chapter=[[w:Richard II of England|Richarde the Seconde]]|page=351|passage=And if any manner of perſon attempted to moue the King to infringe any parte of thys ordynaunce, and that being knowne, for the firſt time, he ſhould be depriued of his goodes and poſſeſſions, and for the ſecond time, to be '''drawen''' thorough the Citie, and ſo put to execution as an arrant traytor.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author={{w|Robert Barr (writer)|Robert Barr}}|chapter=4|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1796924W Lord Stranleigh Abroad]|passage=“[…] No rogue e’er felt the halter '''draw''', with a good opinion of the law, and perhaps my own detestation of the law arises from my having frequently broken it.{{nb...}}”}}
##* {{RQ:Burroughs Land That Time Forgot|chapter=VIII|passage=Lys shuddered, and I put my arm around her and '''drew''' her to me; and thus we sat throughout the hot night. She told me of her abduction and of the fright she had undergone, and together we thanked God that she had come through unharmed, because the great brute had dared not pause along the danger-infested way.}}
##* {{RQ:Orwell Animal Farm|chapter=1|passage=At the last moment Mollie, the foolish, pretty white mare who '''drew''' Mr. Jones's trap, came mincing daintily in, chewing at a lump of sugar.}}
## {{lb|en|archery}} To pull back (the [[string]] of a [[bow]]) in preparation for shooting. {{defdate|from 12th c.}}
## To [[move]] (a part of one's body) in a particular direction. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{usex|en|She settled in the window seat, '''drawing''' her leg up beneath her.}}
## To [[pull]] (a curtain, blinds etc.) [[open]] or [[closed]]. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|You should '''draw''' the curtains at night.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1944|month=November and December|title="Duplex Roomette" Sleeping Cars|journal=Railway Magazine|page=324|text=It is realised that the old Pullman standard sleeper, with its convertible "sections", each containing upper and lower berths, and with no greater privacy at night than the curtains '''drawn''' along both sides of a middle aisle, has had its day.}}
##: {{ux|en|She '''drew''' the curtains to let in the sunlight.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|now|rare}} To pull something along; to have force to move anything by pulling. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|This horse '''draws''' well.}}
## To [[pull]] (one's face, features) out of shape, from emotion etc. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 8|act=IV|scene=ii|passage=How long her face is '''drawn'''!}}
## {{lb|en|now|rare}} To [[construct]] (a wall, canal etc.) from one point to another. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1874|author=w:John Richard Green|title=w:A Short History of the English People
|passage=the huge Offa's dike which he '''drew''' from the mouth of Wye to that of Dee}}
## To require (a depth of water) for floating. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|A ship '''draws''' ten feet of water.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Troilus and Cressida|act=II|scene=iii|passage=Greater hulks '''draw''' deep.}}
## {{lb|en|reflexive}} To [[assume]] a specific position or attitude. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##: {{usex|en|He '''drew''' himself to his full height and glowered at the interloper.}}
## To [[pull]] (a belt or other item) so that it [[tighten]]s or wraps around something more closely. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##: {{usex|en|She took a deep breath and '''drew''' her corset-strings.}}
## {{lb|en|curling}} To make a shot that lands gently in the [[house]] (the circular target) without knocking out other [[stone]]s. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|now|rare}} To be [[pulled]] along (in a specified way). {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The carriage '''draws''' easily.}}
## {{lb|en|cricket}} To play (a short-length ball directed at the leg stump) with an inclined bat so as to deflect the ball between the legs and the wicket. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
## {{lb|en|golf}} To hit (the ball) with the toe of the club so that it is deflected toward the left. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2013|author=Nick Bradley|title=Kinetic Golf
|passage={{...}} the golfer thinks, “Ah! To '''draw''' the ball, I must be like the old pros and incorporate hand action through impact, twisting the clubhead to the left.” All you have to do to apply the spin needed to '''draw''' the ball left is to create a very small conflict between the clubface aim and the path the clubhead is traveling.}}
## {{lb|en|billiards}} To strike (the cue ball) below the center so as to give it a backward rotation which causes it to take a backward direction on striking another ball. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
# To attract, exert an influence on.
## To [[induce]] (the mind, eyes, attention etc.) to be directed at or focused on something. {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
##: {{usex|en|From the moment she entered the room, all eyes were '''drawn''' to her.}}
##: {{usex|en|His mind was '''drawn''' back to the events of the preceding morning.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1964|month=April|title=Letters: Rethinking emergency procedures|journal=Modern Railways|page=274|text=Handsignalmen, where needed, ought to wear a conspicuous orange/yellow cape (like many road workmen) to '''draw''' attention to them.}}
## To cause (someone) to come to a particular place, condition, or course of action; to [[attract]] (a person). {{defdate|from 12th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=5|passage=A place like the Right Livers' Rest was bound to '''draw''' freaks, same as molasses '''draws''' flies.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1935|author=[https://www.goodreads.com/author/show/288354.George_Goodchild George Goodchild]|title=Death on the Centre Court|chapter=5|passage=The restaurant was packed, and the promenade between the two main courts and the subsidiary courts was thronged with healthy-looking youngish people, '''drawn''' to the Mecca of tennis from all parts of the country.}}
## To take (air, smoke etc.) into the [[lung]]s; to [[inhale]]. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|I '''drew''' a deep breath and wiped my brow.}}
##* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=I|passage=Serene, smiling, enigmatic, she faced him with no fear whatever showing in her dark eyes.{{...}}She put back a truant curl from her forehead where it had sought egress to the world, and looked him full in the face now, '''drawing''' a deep breath which caused the round of her bosom to lift the lace at her throat.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1979|author=w:Monty Python|title=w:Always Look on the Bright Side of Life
|passage=So always look on the bright side of death, / Just before you '''draw''' your terminal breath.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To [[attract]] (something) by means of a physical force, especially magnetism or gravity; {{lb|en|figurative}} to act as an inducement or enticement. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|passage=These following bodies do not '''draw''': smaragd, achates, corneolus, pearl, jaspis, chalcedonius, alabaster, porphyry, coral, marble, touchstone, haematites, or bloodstone {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Spectator|II|128|27 July 1711|passage=Keep a watch upon the particular bias which nature has fixed in their minds, that it may not '''draw''' too much.}}
## To [[cause]] (something); to bring (something) about as a consequence. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=July 3, 2011|author=Piers Newbury|work=BBC Sport|title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/tennis/14006575.stm Wimbledon 2011: Novak Djokovic beats Rafael Nadal in final]|passage=In a desperately tight opening set, the pace and accuracy of the Serbian's groundstrokes began to '''draw''' errors from the usually faultless Nadal and earned him the first break point of the day at 5-4.}}
## To [[provoke]] or [[attract]] (a particular response or reaction). {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The president's comments have '''drawn''' strong criticism from rightwing media outlets.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To have a [[draught]]; to allow air to be passed through in order to allow for [[combustion]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The chimney won't '''draw''' properly if it's clogged up with soot.}}
# ''To extend, protract.''
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} To extend the [[duration]] of (something); to [[prolong]]. {{defdate|10th–19th c.}}
## To make (wire) by pulling it through an aperture; to [[stretch]] (metal) into a wire. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''draw''' a mass of metal into wire}}
## To [[stretch]] or [[elongate]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The dough was run through the pasta machine and '''drawn''' into a long ribbon.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[contracted]]; to [[shrink]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|passage=water{{...}} will shrink or '''draw''' into less room}}
## {{lb|en|nautical}} Of a sail, to [[fill]] with wind. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|A ship's sail is said to '''draw''' when it is filled with wind.}}
# To move, travel, approach.
## {{lb|en|reflexive|now|rare}} To [[move]] in a specific direction. {{defdate|from 12th c.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1794|author=w:Ann Radcliffe|title=The Mysteries of Udolpho
|passage=She thought she heard a noise in her chamber, and she '''drew''' herself within the casement.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|used with prepositions and adverbs}} To move steadily in a particular direction or into a specific position. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The runners '''drew''' level with each other as they approached the finish line.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''Draw''' near to the fire and I will tell you a tale.}}
##: {{ux|en|The end of the world '''draws''' near.}}
##: {{ux|en|Heavy clouds '''drew''' together above our heads.}}
## To [[come]] {{m|en|to}}, {{m|en|towards}} (a particular moment in time); to [[approach]] (a time). {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|As it '''drew''' towards evening, I packed up and headed for home.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=October|title=The Victoria Line was only part of the plan|journal=Modern Railways|page=258|text=As the war '''drew''' to its end, it became evident that repairs and rebuilding in the heavily blitzed Greater London area would be so extensive as to afford opportunity for effective large-scale planning.}}
## {{lb|en|hunting|now|rare}} To [[search]] for game; to [[track]] a quarry. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1928|author=w:Siegfried Sassoon|title=w:Memoirs of a Fox-Hunting Man|page=87|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2013
|passage=On one of my expeditions, after a stormy night, at the end of March, the hounds '''drew''' all day without finding a fox.}}
# To extract, remove, select.
## To [[pull out]], [[unsheathe]] (a sword, firearm etc.). {{defdate|from 12th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|They '''drew''' their swords and fought each other.}}
## To take (water) from a well or other source. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''draw''' water from a well}}
##* {{RQ:King James Version|John|4|11|passage=The woman saith unto him, Sir, thou hast nothing to '''draw''' with, and the well is deep.}}
## To [[disembowel]] (someone); to remove the [[viscera]] from (an animal), especially before cooking. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1709|author={{w|William King (poet)|William King}}|title=The Art of Cookery
|passage=In private '''draw''' your poultry, clean your tripe.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|medicine|now|rare}} To [[extract]] (pus, humours, etc.) by means of medical treatment. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
## To [[select]] (an item) at random to decide which of a group of people will receive or undergo something; to [[select]] (a person) by this process. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1784|author=w:Edward Augustus Freeman|url=https://archive.org/details/essayonparliamen00edinuoft|title=An essay on parliamentary representation, and the magistracies of our boroughs royal:{{nb...}}
|passage=Provided magistracies were filled by men freely chosen or '''drawn'''.}}
## To conduct (a lottery); to select (the numbers) for a lottery; to [[win]] (a prize) in a lottery. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The winning lottery numbers were '''drawn''' every Tuesday.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1859|author=w:Charles Dickens|title=The Haunted House
|passage=In the drawing of lots, my sister '''drew''' her own room, and I drew Master B.'s.}}
## To [[extract]] (a tooth). {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
## To [[extract]] (juice, fluids etc.) from something by pressure, osmosis or similar. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1705|author={{w|George Cheyne (physician)|George Cheyne}}|title=Philosophical Principles of Religion Natural and Revealed
|passage=Spirits, by distillations, may be '''drawn''' out of vegetable juices, which shall flame and fume of themselves.}}
## {{lb|en|cards}} To take or be dealt (a card) from the deck; to have (a particular hand) as a result of this. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|At the start of their turn, each player must '''draw''' a card.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[withdraw]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|act=II|scene=i|passage=Go, wash thy face, and '''draw''' thy action.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|or|intransitive}} To end a game in a [[#Noun|draw]] (with neither side winning). {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|We '''drew''' last time we played.&emsp; I '''drew''' him last time I played him.&emsp; I '''drew''' my last game against him.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|year_published='''2010'''|edition=HTML|author=w:Edgar Rice Burroughs|title=[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/1153/1153-h/1153-h.htm The Chessmen of Mars]|publisher=The Gutenberg Project|passage=The game is won when a player places any of his pieces on the same square with his opponent's Princess, or when a Chief takes a Chief. It is '''drawn''' when a Chief is taken by any opposing piece other than the opposing Chief;{{nb...}}}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|transitive}} To [[steep]]; to leave (tea) temporarily in water to allow the [[flavour]] to increase. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Lindsay Redheap|page=207|passage="There's your tay set for you an' '''drawin'''' nicely this minute, Miss Ethel," called old Bridget from the hall.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1984|author=w:Anita Brookner|title=Hotel du Lac|page=119|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2016
|passage=She made a pot of very strong tea, and while she was waiting for it to '''draw''' she opened the kitchen door to inspect her garden.}}
##: {{ux|en|Tea is much nicer if you let it '''draw''' for more than two minutes before pouring.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[take]] or be dealt a [[playing card]] from the deck. ''See also'' '''[[draw out]]'''.
##: {{ux|en|Jill has four diamonds; she'll try to '''draw''' for a flush.}}
## To [[run]] (a bath). {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
## {{lb|en|analogous}} To consume ([[power]]).
##: {{ux|en|The circuit '''draws''' three hundred watts.}}
# To obtain, elicit.
## To [[take]] (something) from a particular source, especially of information; to [[derive]]. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|He '''drew''' comfort from the thought that he was not the first to suffer this way.}}
##: {{ux|en|She '''draws''' her subject matter from the events of her own life.}}
## To [[call forth]] (something) from a person, to [[elicit]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Winter's Tale|act=I|scene=ii|passage=I had thought, sir, to have held my peace until<br>You have '''drawn''' oaths from him not to stay.}}
## To [[deduce]] or [[infer]] (a conclusion); to make (a deduction). {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|He tried to '''draw''' a conclusion from the facts.}}
##* {{RQ:Burke Revolution in France|passage=We do not '''draw''' the moral lessons we might from history.}}
## To [[receive]] (a salary); to [[withdraw]] (money) from a bank etc. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''draw''' money from a bank}}
## To [[elicit]] information from (someone); to induce (a person) to speak {{m|en|on}} some subject. (Now frequently in passive.) {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|He refused to be '''drawn''' on the subject}}
# To represent.
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[produce]] (a shape, figure, picture etc.) with [[pencil]], [[crayon]], [[chalk]], or other implement. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|A crude picture had been '''drawn''' on the wall of his poky apartment.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[depict]] (something) linguistically; to [[portray]] in [[word]]s. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Her first novel contained a host of characters who were richly and convincingly '''drawn'''.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[draw up]], [[compose]] (a document). {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''draw''' a memorial, a deed, or bill of exchange}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|act=IV|scene=i|passage=Clerk, '''draw''' a deed of gift.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To produce an image of something by artistic means; to make drawings. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|When I came in she was '''drawing''' on a big piece of coloured paper.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To produce a visual representation of (a person or thing) by lines and marks with pencil, pen, paints etc. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Goldsmith Retaliation|page=10|passage=A flattering painter, vvho made it his care / To '''dravv''' men as they ought to be, not as they are.}}
##* {{RQ:Prior Poems|year=1718|poem=To Mr. Howard|page=70|stanza=I|passage=Can I untouch'd the Fair ones Paſſions move? / Or Thou '''draw''' Beauty, and not feel it's Pow'r?}}
##* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=III|passage=Sepia Delft tiles surrounded the fireplace, their crudely '''drawn''' Biblical scenes in faded cyclamen blending with the pinkish pine, while above them, instead of a mantelshelf, there was an archway high enough to form a balcony with slender balusters and a tapestry-hung wall behind.}}

